   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">WFP&apos;S RESULTS GLOBALLY IN 2021

WFPs top ten donors accounted for 79 percent of contributions. Sweden provided 1.54 billion SEK to WFP in2021, including core support from MFA and humanitarian and development assistance from Sida strategies. This sum was surpassed in 2022 when the total amount increased to 2.54 billion SEK, mainly procurements in Ukraine through the Black Sea Graine Initiative. Sida HUM provided 519 MSEK to WFP in 2021, of which 49 MSEK was for UNHAS, and 553.5 MSEK in 2022, of which 79 MSEK was for UNHAS.

In 2021, WFP and its partners worked in 83 countries, reaching a record of 128.2 million people facing acute food insecurity, of which 68 million were women and girls and more than 60 million were men and boys. WFP distributed 4.4 million metric tonne of food and USD 2.3 billion in cash-based transfers, reaching 8 percent more beneficiaries through food assistance and 9 percent more through cash-based transfers and commodity vouchers than in 2020, with expenditures of USD 8.6 billion. WFP received record contributions of USD 9.6 billion, including a surge in private sector funding&quot; which was 15 percent more than in 2020. 

Additionally, UNHAS reached 496 destinations (regular  ad hoc), completed 46,145 flights, transported 325,112 passengers and 5,862 mt of cargo. UNHAS used 75 aircraft (59 fixed-wing and 16 helicopters), performed 616 MEDEVACs (including 50 COVID-19 patients) and 2,399 security related evacuations. UNHAS received MUSD 248 in donor contributions. Sweden, through Sida, was the eighth largest donor to UNHAS in 2021, however as a large donor to the Country-based Pooled Funds (CBPF) and to the Central Emergency Relief Fund (CERF), Sweden&apos;s total contributions to UNHAS are far more significant.

Sida is impressed by the number of persons reached and the quantity of assistance that WFP has been able to deliver during 2021 and 2022. These experiences assure us that WFP&apos;s plans to reach people will continue in 2023 and our funding will be utilised for the intended activities in each crisis.  Of note in WFP&apos;s annual reports is the amount of information presented and the explanation about the impact that food and cash assistance has on peoples lives and nutritional levels.

EXAMPLES OF RESULTS FROM WFP&apos;S ANNUAL COUNTRY REPORTS 2021

Afghanistan

In response to the sharp deterioration in food security, WFP significantly scaled-up operations, providing life-saving food and nutrition assistance to 15 million people (51 percent female and 49 percent male). The number of people assisted monthly increased more than fivefold between August and December 2021, with 7.9 million people assisted in December compared to 1.5 million people assisted in August. Overall, this represents a 67 percent increase on beneficiaries reached in 2020 and 14 percent more than planned. Included in the total are 2.1 million children 6-59 months and pregnant and lactating women (PLW) who received malnutrition prevention and treatment services. Furthermore, WFP played an integral role in the humanitarian community, managing the United Nations Humanitarian Air Service (UNHAS). In 2021, UNHAS became even more critical with the suspension of most commercial international flights in August. UNHAS established airbridges between Kabul and Islamabad (Pakistan), Dushanbe (Tajikistan), and Doha (Qatar) to facilitate the movement of humanitarian workers.

Ethiopia

The operating environment in Ethiopia became more challenging and complex as funding decreased, affecting WFPs ability to deliver a response commensurate to reported needs. 2021 saw an increase in security incidents, interference, access constraints, significant delays and halting of crucial activities, such as climate and livelihood initiatives. Despite these challenges, WFP successfully reached 9.83 million girls and boys, women and men in 2021, including 590,013 persons with disabilities (PWD)[2], through distribution of 447,460 mt of food and USD 18.6 million [3] in cash transfers across its activities in Ethiopia. Collaborating with five partners, WFP reached over 6.3 million people with relief assistance under the HRP in the Northern Ethiopia and Somali regions, extending its assistance to conflict, drought and flood-affected people in need.

Yemen

Overall, WFP assisted 15.5 million girls and boys, women and men across its activities in Yemen in 2021 and remained WFPs largest operation worldwide. This includes 3.4 million internally displaced persons and, overall, women and girls represented half of those assisted by WFP. The total number of people assisted in Yemen in 2021 marks a 5 percent increase from 2020, supported by generous contributions. WFP had by June 2021 resumed monthly assistance to 10 million people in nine governorates facing the highest food insecurity levels. However, progress remained fragile, and by the end of the year, funding shortfalls had forced WFP to reduce the assistance provided to millions of people in a bid to avoid the interruption of assistance to those most in need. WFP has been providing air transport services for humanitarian workers in Yemen through UNHAS since 2015. Through its fleet of three aircrafts, UNHAS Yemen served 87 entities in 2021, including United Nations agencies, international non-governmental organizations (INGOs) and diplomatic missions. As COVID-19-related travel restrictions eased, UNHAS operated 796 flights and transported 13,600 passengers in 2021, more than twice as many as in 2020. On average, 1,130 passengers were carried on 62 flights each month to and from Aden, Addis Ababa, Amman, Djibouti, Marib, Al Mukalla, Sanaa and Sayun.</narrative>
